<Article title="Dead Hang Times by Age Chart: Men & Women" description="Understand dead hang times by age, the limits of published benchmarks and how to compare your own holds. Practical milestones without invented percentiles." schema={'<script type="application/ld+json">' + JSON.stringify(schema) + '</script>'}>
<div class="article-header"><div class="article-breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">/</span>Times by age</div><h1 class="article-h1">Dead hang time by age: what does your result mean?</h1><p class="article-subtitle">Your age alone does not set a required hang time. The research cited here measures handgrip force, not timed hangs. Use your own repeatable baseline and treat training milestones as goals, not population rankings.</p></div>
<div class="article-body">
<div class="button-row"><a class="dh-btn" href="/dead-hang-calculator/">Compare my hold</a><a class="text-link" href="/training-log/">Start a training log</a></div>
<div class="evidence-note"><p><strong>Benchmark correction, 11 September 2026.</strong> Earlier versions of this page presented age-specific seconds and percentiles as established norms. We could not substantiate those claims from the cited sources. They have been removed. This page now separates published grip-strength evidence from personal training goals.</p></div>
<h2 id="average-times">Dead hang times by age: what the chart can tell you</h2>
<p>An age chart is only as useful as the test behind it. The same person can record different hang times on different bars or with different assistance. Keep the testing conditions consistent before interpreting a change.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Age and hang-time interpretation"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Age group</th><th scope="col">What to use</th><th scope="col">What not to infer</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[['Adults under 40','Your own baseline with a consistent grip and bar.','An exact percentile from a generic internet seconds chart.'],['Adults 40–59','Comparable holds and a record of assistance used.','A fixed decline in hanging seconds each decade.'],['Adults 60 and over','A controlled, suitable variation and individual advice where needed.','A required unassisted hold or a lifespan prediction.']].map(([age,use,avoid])=><tr><th scope="row">{age}</th><td>{use}</td><td>{avoid}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="male-standards">Men’s times and women’s times</h2>
<p id="female-standards">Published handgrip-force norms differ by age and sex. Those measurements use a dynamometer and report force. Supporting bodyweight from a bar for time involves another task. Applying the force percentiles directly to hang duration would be misleading.</p>
<p><a href="https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0113637">Dodds et al. explain the instruments and populations in their study</a>. That paper does not provide the age-specific dead-hang seconds previously displayed here.</p>
<h2 id="percentile-chart">Why we do not give dead-hang percentiles</h2>
<p>A percentile needs an appropriate comparison population and a standardised test. We do not have that dataset for these timed hangs. A result such as 60 seconds cannot truthfully become “top 25%” simply by assigning a band to it.</p>
<p>The calculator instead shows what fraction of your own target you have reached. A 30-second hold against a 60-second target is 50% of that target. It says nothing about how many people you outperform.</p>
<h2 id="is-my-time-good">Is your time useful to build from?</h2>
<p>A controlled hold gives you a starting record. A feet-supported hold is also recordable; keep it separate from an unassisted hold. Short holds are not failures. End the attempt while you can step down safely.</p>
<p>10, 30, 60 and 120 seconds are convenient round-number milestones. They are not validated age cut-offs or required fitness standards. Choose your next target and use the <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal hang calculator</a> to compare it.</p>
<h2 id="age-decline">What changes when you repeat the test?</h2>
<p>Record your bar, grip and assistance consistently. A different bar diameter, chalk or foot support changes the conditions. Compare like with like. A lower result on one day is not a diagnosis of declining health.</p>
<h2 id="improve">Turn your baseline into a routine</h2>
<p>Choose manageable holds and enough rest to maintain control. Record what you actually held instead of saving the timer setting as a result. The <a href="/training-programs/">training plans</a> organise practice and review points without promising a particular gain.</p>
